WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.680 position:63%
- This is liquid helium.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.550 position:63%
It's currently sitting at
negative 269 degrees Celsius.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.490 position:63%
That's just four degrees
above absolute zero.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.133 position:63%
But watch what happens if
you start cooling it down.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.453 position:63%
It starts to boil violently.

00:00:15.453 --> 00:00:17.490 position:63%
- (laughs) That's aggressive.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.010 position:63%
- [Derek] There
are bubbles everywhere.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.580 position:63%
That is until 2.17 Kelvin.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.620 position:63%
At that point, something strange happens.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.370 position:63%
(dramatic suspenseful music)

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.090 position:63%
The helium has transformed
into a superfluid.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.693 position:63%
- Ooh. It's completely still again.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.570 position:63%
- What we've got is a beaker

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.750 position:63%
with a leak tight aerogel base.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.930 position:63%
- [Derek] This beaker holds
the liquid helium well,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.600 position:63%
but the moment it turns superfluid,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.000 position:63%
it starts to leak through.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.920 position:63%
- Oh, the transition below is happening.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:53.776 position:63%
Yeah, it's dripping.

00:00:53.776 --> 00:00:55.133 position:63%
- Yeah, it's dripping on the right.

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:57.840 position:63%
(inspirational music)

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.140 position:63%
- [Derek] And the colder it
gets, the faster it pours.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.540 position:63%
- Oh wow! That's gorgeous.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.043 position:63%
So fast.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.203 position:63%
- That's spectacular.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.430 position:63%
superfluid helium can do
things that seem impossible.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.780 position:63%
If you put it in a cup,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.110 position:63%
a thin film climbs up and over the walls,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.330 position:63%
so it empties itself.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.250 position:63%
You can also use superfluid helium

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.190 position:63%
to create a fountain with no moving parts.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.130 position:63%
It is powered solely by light.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.530 position:63%
Scientists spent 30 years
looking at this liquid

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.810 position:63%
without realizing what they had created.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.970 position:63%
- This is really one
of the great phenomenon

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.620 position:63%
in 20th century physics.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.870 position:63%
- And it makes you look at
matter in a different way.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.300 position:63%
It allows us to explore the boundary

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.090 position:63%
of quantum and regular matter.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.310 position:63%
So we teamed up with
the gravity laboratory

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.370 position:63%
at Nottingham University
to recreate the experiments

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.560 position:63%
and to find out what really happens

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.601 position:63%
when you just keep cooling down.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.930 position:63%
The obsession with cooling things down

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.470 position:63%
as much as possible began in the 1800s.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.870 position:63%
People noticed that when
you let a gas expand

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.080 position:63%
against a piston, its temperature drops.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.300 position:63%
That's because as the
particles hit the piston,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.710 position:63%
they give it a little push

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.500 position:63%
and transfer some of their
energy to the piston.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.983 position:63%
So the particles slow down and
their temperature decreases.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.500 position:63%
Lord Kelvin learned of this

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.070 position:63%
and realized it offered a
tantalizing possibility,

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.413 position:63%
that of an absolute temperature scale.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.600 position:63%
Imagine that you let the
gas expand an extreme amount

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.110 position:63%
to the point where all the
particles effectively stop.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.377 position:63%
The particles no longer
exert any force on the piston

00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:43.800 position:63%
and they have no more energy left to give.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.430 position:63%
This would be the absolute
coldest you could get,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.053 position:63%
absolute zero.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.720 position:63%
Kelvin quickly computed what
temperature it corresponded to

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.010 position:63%
and found it was around
negative 273 degrees Celsius.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.470 position:63%
People speculated what strange things

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.460 position:63%
might happen down there, a
place with no thermal motion.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.380 position:63%
Kelvin himself suggested that metals,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.990 position:63%
which are normally great
conductors of electricity,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.150 position:63%
might turn into perfect insulators

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.360 position:63%
and not conduct any electricity at all.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.060 position:63%
But there was only one way to find out

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.790 position:63%
and that was you had to get there.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.890 position:63%
Fortunately, the race to absolute zero

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.080 position:63%
was already well underway.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.530 position:63%
It had started by
accident 25 years earlier.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.200 position:63%
In 1823, Michael Faraday
was playing around

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.790 position:63%
with chlorine hydrate

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.370 position:63%
and his scientific mentor
suggested that heating it

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.660 position:63%
in a sealed tube might lead
to some interesting results.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.660 position:63%
So Faraday sealed it
up, warmed it in water,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.360 position:63%
and watched as a yellow
atmosphere emerged.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.450 position:63%
Then as the pressure increased
to around four atmospheres,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.600 position:63%
he noticed that a bright
yellow fluid formed.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.683 position:63%
He had just turned chlorine
gas into chlorine liquid.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.130 position:63%
Faraday then went on a
bit of a liquefying spree,

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.250 position:63%
liquefying six more
gases within five weeks.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:09.270 position:63%
But just as he was hitting
his stride, he hit a wall.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.630 position:63%
Some gases like ethylene
refused to liquefy

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.853 position:63%
no matter how much he squeezed them.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:19.440 position:63%
To see why you need to zoom into a gas.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.750 position:63%
There you'd find a bunch of
particles whizzing around

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.313 position:63%
as fast as tiny bullets.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.980 position:63%
And while it might not seem like it,

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.963 position:63%
the particles also attract each other.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.660 position:63%
It's just that they're so spread out

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.940 position:63%
that they meet relatively little,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.710 position:63%
and when they do, they're going so fast

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.080 position:63%
that they mostly just
bounce off each other.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.170 position:63%
In some cases though, if you
squeeze the particles together

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.080 position:63%
by increasing the pressure,
you can get those particles

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.360 position:63%
to stick together and
slide past each other.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.550 position:63%
In other words, you get a liquid.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.040 position:63%
This is what happened
with Faraday's chlorine,

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.710 position:63%
but that doesn't work for all gases.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.050 position:63%
If the attraction between
particles isn't strong enough

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.700 position:63%
and the particles are too hot,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.780 position:63%
then no amount of squeezing
can get them to liquefy.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.540 position:63%
And this is what happened with ethylene.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.583 position:63%
Luckily, there is another way.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.720 position:63%
If you cool the gas down,
the particles will slow

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.940 position:63%
and eventually they
start sticking together

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.453 position:63%
and sliding past each other
and you get your liquid.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.320 position:63%
So to liquefy more tricky gases,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.100 position:63%
Faraday needed colder temperatures
and he got a lucky break.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.010 position:63%
A French inventor had
discovered how to make dry ice,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.980 position:63%
which was about negative
79 degrees Celsius.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.860 position:63%
So 20 years after his
initial chlorine success,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.120 position:63%
he tried again.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.570 position:63%
Faraday put ethylene gas in
a glass tube with a bend,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.100 position:63%
compressed it using pumps,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.970 position:63%
and then lowered the bend into
a bath of dry ice and ether.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.440 position:63%
As the dry ice cooled,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.980 position:63%
the ethylene, a clear
colorless liquid formed.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.630 position:63%
Faraday used the same technique

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.340 position:63%
and liquefied five more gases

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.160 position:63%
pursuing ever colder
temperatures in the process

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.640 position:63%
and getting all the way down
to negative 110 degrees Celsius

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.710 position:63%
or 163 Kelvin.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.560 position:63%
But six gases still resisted his attempts,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.230 position:63%
including oxygen, hydrogen and nitrogen.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.870 position:63%
He just couldn't get cold enough.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.190 position:63%
Over the following decades,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.110 position:63%
scientists all over the world picked up

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.880 position:63%
where Faraday had left off.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.200 position:63%
Liquefying a new gas became
a matter of national pride

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.053 position:63%
and the race to absolute zero was on.

00:06:20.209 --> 00:06:24.210 position:63%
In 1883, two Polish scientists
liquefied oxygen and nitrogen

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.600 position:63%
and the record low
temperature fell to 77 Kelvin.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.620 position:63%
15 years later, James Dewar
liquified hydrogen at 20 Kelvin

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.350 position:63%
before freezing it at 14.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.970 position:63%
By the turn of the 20th century,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:39.810 position:63%
there was just one gas
left standing, helium,

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:43.740 position:63%
but some thought helium might
be impossible to liquefy.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.480 position:63%
And there was good reason to think that

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.970 position:63%
because to turn a gas into a liquid,

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.670 position:63%
its particles have to attract each other.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.280 position:63%
But helium is a very simple element.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.010 position:63%
Its most common isotope
consists of two protons,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.380 position:63%
two neutrons and two electrons.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.440 position:63%
Electrons form shells around
the nucleus and helium,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.010 position:63%
like all noble gases,
has a full outer shell.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.550 position:63%
So it isn't really seeking to
gain or lose any electrons,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.810 position:63%
and as a result,

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:12.570 position:63%
it doesn't really bind
together with anything.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.130 position:63%
That made people wonder

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.950 position:63%
whether helium atoms
attract each other at all.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.500 position:63%
And if they didn't, you
could cool down helium

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.770 position:63%
as much as you wanted, but
you would never get a liquid.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.440 position:63%
And even if the atoms
did attract each other,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.180 position:63%
well, the attraction might be so small

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.450 position:63%
that its liquefaction
temperature could be much colder

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.553 position:63%
than anyone could practically reach.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.560 position:63%
So when Heike Kamerlingh
Onnes set his sights

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.460 position:63%
on liquefying helium, he knew
he was in for a challenge.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.910 position:63%
Onnes had joined the race
to absolute zero in 1882,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.320 position:63%
and since then he'd been building

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.110 position:63%
an industrial scale laboratory in Leiden,

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.930 position:63%
complete with chemists,
technicians, glassblowers,

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.060 position:63%
and massive liquefiers.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.970 position:63%
By 1906, he too had liquefied hydrogen,

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.610 position:63%
and from then on, he
dedicated himself fully

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:00.903 position:63%
to liquefying helium.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:06.750 position:63%
But working with helium came
with two major problems.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.480 position:63%
For one thing, just
getting it was difficult.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.060 position:63%
Remember, helium doesn't
react with anything,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.480 position:63%
so there are no compounds
you can get helium from,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.170 position:63%
and the air only contains
around five parts per million.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.290 position:63%
So on earth, there's almost no helium,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.210 position:63%
which is at least a little surprising

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.970 position:63%
because in the universe,
helium is everywhere.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.060 position:63%
It's the second most abundant
element after hydrogen.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.150 position:63%
The issue is just how light helium is.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.330 position:63%
Release any helium and
it mixes into the air.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.960 position:63%
Over hundreds of thousands
of years, it slowly drifts up

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.900 position:63%
to the top of the atmosphere
where it gets heated up

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.910 position:63%
by the sun and it speeds up.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.100 position:63%
Now, a small fraction ends up going faster

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.960 position:63%
than 11 kilometers per second,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.560 position:63%
which is Earth's escape
velocity, the velocity required

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.080 position:63%
to escape Earth's gravitational well.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.720 position:63%
And so it escapes into space.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.870 position:63%
A much larger fraction gets
ionized by solar radiation

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.100 position:63%
and then gets carried away

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.830 position:63%
by the polar winds along
the Earth's magnetic field

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.320 position:63%
before ultimately merging
with the solar wind.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.777 position:63%
And either way, the outcome is the same.

00:09:09.777 --> 00:09:13.020 position:63%
The helium is lost to space forever.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.972 position:63%
So if I pop this balloon, that
helium is effectively gone.

00:09:16.972 --> 00:09:18.540 position:63%
(balloon pops)

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:22.320 position:63%
As a result, pure helium at
the time was hard to come by

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.930 position:63%
and the world's total
supply was likely less

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.570 position:63%
than 50 party balloons worth.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:31.080 position:63%
So Onnes had to get his
helium from a different source

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.750 position:63%
and he turned to sand,

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.800 position:63%
specifically two large
bags of Monazite sand.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.450 position:63%
That's because Monazite is a mineral

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.780 position:63%
that contains around 5% thorium by weight

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.240 position:63%
and thorium is radioactive.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.880 position:63%
When it decays, it spits
out an alpha particle,

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.310 position:63%
which is two protons and two neutrons.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.190 position:63%
In other words, the nucleus of helium.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:56.250 position:63%
That nucleus then gets slowed
down by the surrounding rock

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.330 position:63%
where it steals two electrons
and becomes a helium atom.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.940 position:63%
So over millions of years,
you get this buildup

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.450 position:63%
of helium atoms trapped inside the rock.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.210 position:63%
In fact, basically all the
helium we have on earth today

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.370 position:63%
comes from radioactive decay.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.800 position:63%
It's essentially a radioactive fossil,

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.740 position:63%
although now we mostly get
it from natural gas wells

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.990 position:63%
where it accumulates over the eons.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.330 position:63%
But Onnes didn't have that luxury.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.643 position:63%
He had to liberate it from the sand.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.790 position:63%
So he recruited four chemists.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:29.790 position:63%
They heated up the sand
to release the gas,

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:31.620 position:63%
exploded it with oxygen,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.560 position:63%
cooled it with liquid air
to freeze out impurities,

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.770 position:63%
burned over copper oxide
to get rid of the hydrogen,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:40.320 position:63%
and last of all,
compressed it over charcoal

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.840 position:63%
to absorb any remaining impurities.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.550 position:63%
After several months,

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:48.330 position:63%
Onnes had about 25 party
balloons of pure helium,

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:51.543 position:63%
an estimated 80% of the world supply.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.810 position:63%
Onnes quickly conducted
careful experiments

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.190 position:63%
to try and tease out

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.500 position:63%
whether helium atoms
attract each other at all.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.037 position:63%
- Kamerlingh Onnes did a lot of work

00:11:02.037 --> 00:11:05.760 position:63%
and I think he was becoming
more and more convinced

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.610 position:63%
that helium can in fact be liquefied.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.490 position:63%
- It seemed like the helium
atoms did attract each other

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.440 position:63%
just ever so slightly.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.840 position:63%
So how do two neutral atoms

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.540 position:63%
that don't react with
anything attract each other?

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.460 position:63%
Well, a helium atom is just a nucleus

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.280 position:63%
with two electrons around
it, but in reality,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.980 position:63%
you never know exactly
where those electrons are.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.800 position:63%
So a more accurate
picture looks like this,

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.980 position:63%
a nucleus with an
electron cloud around it.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.160 position:63%
But that cloud isn't always
perfectly evenly distributed.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.540 position:63%
At any particular instant,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.150 position:63%
there's slightly more
negative charge on one side

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.200 position:63%
than the other.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.930 position:63%
And now if there's another
helium atom close enough,

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:46.590 position:63%
well, this charge imbalance
can induce a charge imbalance

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.270 position:63%
on that atom.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:52.890 position:63%
And now the two exert a force
of attraction on each other.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:56.310 position:63%
This is known as a van der Waals force,

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.400 position:63%
and it's why every atom
attracts every other

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.230 position:63%
if you get it close enough.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:04.200 position:63%
But helium holds its two
electrons very tightly,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.910 position:63%
so the cloud hardly budges

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.920 position:63%
and the van der Waals force is tiny,

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:12.483 position:63%
the smallest of any element,
but perhaps it's just enough.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.360 position:63%
The second problem with helium is that

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.150 position:63%
no one knew exactly how
cold it would have to get

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:20.670 position:63%
to liquefy, only that it had to be colder

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.310 position:63%
than liquid hydrogen,
possibly much colder.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:24.780 position:63%
And that's the issue

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:27.780 position:63%
because the rate at
which heat transfers in

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.690 position:63%
from the environment depends
on the temperature difference.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.810 position:63%
The bigger the gap, the more heat flows in

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:36.690 position:63%
and cooling something down
to possibly a few degrees

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:37.800 position:63%
above absolute zero

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.550 position:63%
while the room it's in
is 290 degrees above it,

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.250 position:63%
well, that's a humongous gap.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.470 position:63%
Fortunately, Dewar had already invented

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.333 position:63%
one key piece of apparatus.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:53.130 position:63%
Essentially, one glass
flask nested inside another

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.320 position:63%
with a vacuum in between.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.000 position:63%
This limited heat conduction

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:59.460 position:63%
to a small connection point at the top.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.640 position:63%
Convection got ruled out
entirely due to the vacuum

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.920 position:63%
and optionally by covering the insides

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:08.310 position:63%
with a silver coating, you
could also reflect radiation.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:11.400 position:63%
This reduced heat losses
by a factor of 30,

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.973 position:63%
and it's the basic design behind
every thermos flask today.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:19.473 position:63%
But for Onnes, it still
wasn't good enough.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:24.870 position:63%
So he nested several Dewar
flasks inside each other.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.970 position:63%
The helium would go in the inner one,

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:29.730 position:63%
a bath of liquid hydrogen
would sit around that,

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:32.250 position:63%
and a bath of liquid air around that.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.400 position:63%
So each layer acted as a
heat shield for the next,

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:39.510 position:63%
and the helium would see a wall
of 20 Kelvin instead of 300,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:43.927 position:63%
cutting the radiated heat
coming in by a factor of 50,000.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.310 position:63%
To measure the temperature
inside the inner vessel,

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.680 position:63%
Onnes inserted a small glass bulb,

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:53.013 position:63%
filled with dilute helium gas
held at a constant volume.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.130 position:63%
A thin capillary connected this to

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.313 position:63%
what was essentially a pressure
meter outside the liquefier,

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.580 position:63%
because for a gas at a constant volume,

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:06.150 position:63%
the temperature is directly
proportional to the pressure.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.160 position:63%
So if you first measure
the baseline pressure

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:09.180 position:63%
at a temperature you know,

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.660 position:63%
like the freezing point of
water around 273 Kelvin,

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.443 position:63%
you could set that to 100%.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.000 position:63%
Then if you later measure a pressure

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.890 position:63%
that's only 10% of that amount,

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:22.900 position:63%
then the temperature must
also be 10% of the original

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.683 position:63%
or around 27 Kelvin.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:29.190 position:63%
By using this trick, Onnes
could measure the temperature

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.580 position:63%
even at a few degrees above absolute zero.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:35.520 position:63%
Now in their case, they
read off a level of mercury,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.853 position:63%
but for simplicity, we'll animate
it as a temperature scale.

00:14:39.807 --> 00:14:42.780 position:63%
Onnes finally had everything ready.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.030 position:63%
At Professor Weinfurtner's
Gravity Laboratory,

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.520 position:63%
we got to see the setup we'd use.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.110 position:63%
- [Casper] Is this also what
it would have looked like

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:51.510 position:63%
back in the day when Onnes was doing it?

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:52.440 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] Yeah, so this is actually

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.410 position:63%
a really similar setup
to Kamerlingh Onnes.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:56.490 position:63%
- [Derek] The biggest difference is that

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:59.190 position:63%
we only have two dewars
nested inside each other.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.590 position:63%
The outer one will be
filled with liquid nitrogen

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:03.890 position:63%
and the inner one is
where the helium will go.

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.970 position:63%
By the 10th of July 1908,
Onnes decided to go for it.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:11.400 position:63%
He arrived at his lab around 5:30 AM

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.803 position:63%
where his technicians
were already hard at work.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:18.207 position:63%
They prepared 75 liters of
liquid air the day before

00:15:18.207 --> 00:15:22.350 position:63%
and Onnes now ordered them to
start making liquid hydrogen.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.870 position:63%
By 1:30 PM they had 20 liters,

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.783 position:63%
which was enough to launch
their attack on helium.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.323 position:63%
They started by filling the outer vessels.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.850 position:63%
- First step, we're gonna
fill the outer dewar

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.440 position:63%
with liquid nitrogen.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.300 position:63%
That's going to precool the whole system

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.640 position:63%
down to about 77 Kelvin.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.370 position:63%
Once we're full, we'll
start filling with helium.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:52.370 position:63%
So we've used about 25 liters of nitrogen

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:54.660 position:63%
to get to that point.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:56.880 position:63%
Now consider how big the dewar is,

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.693 position:63%
that probably holds, you
know, less than 10 liters.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:04.440 position:63%
- [Derek] With the liquid
air and hydrogen in place,

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.393 position:63%
they started to cool the liquefier.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.503 position:63%
After 30 minutes, it
had cooled to 93 Kelvin.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.490 position:63%
Now it was time to start
cycling the helium.

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:16.890 position:63%
A pump compressed it to 100 atmospheres

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:18.659 position:63%
before it went to the liquefier,

00:16:18.659 --> 00:16:21.960 position:63%
where it got pre-cooled
to around 15 Kelvin.

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:25.380 position:63%
The gas was then rapidly
expanded into the inner vessel,

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:27.993 position:63%
greatly increasing the
distance between atoms.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.580 position:63%
But remember, helium atoms do
slightly attract each other,

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.037 position:63%
so moving them apart costs energy

00:16:35.037 --> 00:16:38.310 position:63%
and they pay for this with
some of their kinetic energy,

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.230 position:63%
causing the particles to slow down

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.973 position:63%
and their temperature to drop.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:47.010 position:63%
The gas then got pumped
back to the compressor,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.150 position:63%
but the smart thing was that

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.480 position:63%
they let this colder gas
cool the incoming helium,

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.810 position:63%
slightly lowering its
temperature before it expanded.

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.670 position:63%
And by doing this over and over,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:59.643 position:63%
the incoming helium got colder and colder.

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:06.210 position:63%
- [Casper] It's so faint.
- Yeah.

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:08.670 position:63%
- [Casper] It feels like a
ghostly fluid or something.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.500 position:63%
- You know, it's not
liquid that's escaping.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.710 position:63%
It's more of kind of a super dense vapor.

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:17.643 position:63%
It's only 10 degrees inside,
but it's dropping fast.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:20.340 position:63%
- [Derek] A little after 6:30 PM,

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:22.740 position:63%
Onnes and his team had
gotten down to six degrees

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.390 position:63%
above absolute zero.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:28.020 position:63%
That is closer than anyone
had ever gotten before,

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.640 position:63%
and they kept going.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:34.640 position:63%
But just below five Kelvin, the
temperature stopped falling,

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:37.923 position:63%
so they started to worry that
perhaps something was wrong.

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.600 position:63%
But then a chemist in the room
remarked, "It's so stable,

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.270 position:63%
it's almost as if the
thermometer is submerged

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:46.467 position:63%
in a bath of liquid."

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:52.770 position:63%
So they rushed to the
apparatus, but still saw nothing

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:56.730 position:63%
until Onnes grabbed a torch
and shot it from a low angle,

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.863 position:63%
and they saw a reflection.

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:01.530 position:63%
After the surface had once been seen,

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:03.930 position:63%
it was no more lost sight of.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.360 position:63%
Onnes wrote, "It stood out sharply defined

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:09.057 position:63%
like the edge of a knife
against a glass wall."

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.882 position:63%
They had just made liquid helium.

00:18:13.882 --> 00:18:17.340 position:63%
(uplifting music)

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:18.415 position:63%
- That's really cool.

00:18:18.415 --> 00:18:21.030 position:63%
- It's just hitting the light
on it while it's yeah.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:24.124 position:63%
- So beautiful. 
- It's a helium kettle.

00:18:24.124 --> 00:18:26.370 position:63%
(everybody laughs)

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:29.280 position:63%
- Four degrees
above absolute zero.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:31.260 position:63%
- You can see that
really faint liquid level

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.180 position:63%
on the inside, that's the liquid helium.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:34.700 position:63%
- Oh wow. Okay.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.020 position:63%
So the refractive index of liquid helium

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.340 position:63%
is very close to air, is it?

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:39.902 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] 1.025.

00:18:39.902 --> 00:18:42.720 position:63%
Okay. So yeah, really, really
difficult to see by eye.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:43.553 position:63%
- [Derek] Wow.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.470 position:63%
- Isn't it crazy to think that that is

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:49.203 position:63%
about four degrees above absolute zero?

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:51.600 position:63%
It doesn't look like it.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.764 position:63%
- There's nothing
that would tip you off.

00:18:53.764 --> 00:18:55.181 position:63%
- Yeah.

00:18:56.403 --> 00:18:58.920 position:63%
- [Derek] Back in Leiden,
everyone was elated.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.320 position:63%
For the next hour, they
ran basic experiments

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.430 position:63%
until by 8:30 PM only 10
milliliters were left.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.790 position:63%
But then Onnes thought, "Well,
we've made liquid helium.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.980 position:63%
Why not make solid helium?"

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:13.020 position:63%
So he was going to cool it down,

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:15.780 position:63%
and the way you can
cool down any liquid is

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.370 position:63%
by pumping on it.

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:18.330 position:63%
- When we pump on it,

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.640 position:63%
we're removing all of the
hottest atoms from the system,

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.680 position:63%
which takes about a
third of the fluid away.

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.560 position:63%
So we initially take the level
way higher than we need it

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:26.913 position:63%
so it can drop down.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.963 position:63%
- [Derek] So Onnes started
his pumps and so did we.

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:33.720 position:63%
- Are we ready?

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.270 position:63%
- Yeah, on three, two, one, go.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.932 position:63%
- Oh, wow. Okay.

00:19:38.932 --> 00:19:41.932 position:63%
(suspenseful music)

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:46.980 position:63%
- Oh wow.
It's gone down so much.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.300 position:63%
It's gone down like,
what, five centimeters?

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:50.580 position:63%
- Yeah, immediately.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:52.350 position:63%
- Immediately.
- Because we're removing

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:54.393 position:63%
all the hottest atoms from the system.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.960 position:63%
So we're just shy of 3.5 K.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:19:59.820 position:63%
- [Derek] This rapidly evaporating helium

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:01.650 position:63%
is quickly cooling down.

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:03.363 position:63%
- So we're about 2.6 K.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:07.362 position:63%
We're below 2.3 K.

00:20:07.362 --> 00:20:10.230 position:63%
- Okay. Oh, it's
going super fast, super fast.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.980 position:63%
- [Derek] It keeps boiling
until right at 2.17 Kelvin,

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:18.980 position:63%
it abruptly stops and the
liquid goes eerily quiet.

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:20.550 position:63%
There it is.

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:24.900 position:63%
- Ooh. It's completely still again.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:28.140 position:63%
Man, you can hardly see it. It
feels like a mirror surface.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.003 position:63%
It's crazy how smooth that is.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.310 position:63%
- [Derek] Onnes pumped his
helium down to 1.7 Kelvin,

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:39.720 position:63%
colder than outer space,
but it didn't freeze.

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:43.380 position:63%
Instead, it just sat
there, remarkably still.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.330 position:63%
Onnes concluded it must just freeze

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:46.740 position:63%
at some lower temperature,

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:50.970 position:63%
and since everyone was
exhausted, they called it a day.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.871 position:63%
Little did he know what sat
there evaporating in his flask.

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:05.040 position:63%
Onnes still wanted to freeze helium,

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:08.070 position:63%
so over the next two years,
he tried several more times,

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:11.040 position:63%
getting all the way down to one Kelvin,

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.203 position:63%
but all his attempts failed.

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.980 position:63%
This led him to wonder
whether helium will not remain

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:20.643 position:63%
perhaps liquid even if it is
cooled to the absolute zero.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.930 position:63%
And indeed, scientists later
discovered this is true.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:26.340 position:63%
If you take any substance

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.820 position:63%
and keep cooling it down,
eventually it always freezes,

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.040 position:63%
except for helium.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:35.220 position:63%
Somehow it's the only
substance that never freezes

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:37.680 position:63%
by just cooling it down.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.380 position:63%
So Onnes was wise when he gave up

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.120 position:63%
on trying to freeze helium.

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:44.790 position:63%
Instead, he set out to finally
test Kelvin's prediction

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.380 position:63%
that the electrical conductivity

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:49.200 position:63%
of metals should stop near absolute zero.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.450 position:63%
In other words, that their
electrical resistance

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:53.970 position:63%
should become effectively infinite.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.060 position:63%
To do this, he submerged
different metals in liquid helium

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:58.860 position:63%
and measured their resistance.

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:01.020 position:63%
He started with gold and platinum,

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:04.170 position:63%
but their resistance just
kept slowly decreasing

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:06.843 position:63%
until finally settling on a steady value.

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:11.910 position:63%
Then on the 8th of April,
1911, he measured mercury

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:14.767 position:63%
and wrote down just three words,

00:22:14.767 --> 00:22:15.867 position:63%
"Kwik nagenoeg nul",

00:22:17.257 --> 00:22:19.497 position:63%
"Mercury practically zero."

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.700 position:63%
As you cooled down, its
resistance slowly decreased

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.430 position:63%
until around 4.2 Kelvin,

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:30.750 position:63%
where it suddenly plummets
to what appeared to be zero.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:34.320 position:63%
Electricity that flowed
with no apparent losses,

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:37.800 position:63%
the exact opposite of what
Kelvin had hypothesized,

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:40.593 position:63%
Onnes had just discovered
superconductivity.

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:45.000 position:63%
- So that's the story most people know.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:45.833 position:63%
- Okay.

00:22:45.833 --> 00:22:48.990 position:63%
- What most people don't know
is that later that same day,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.327 position:63%
he also cooled down the
helium more and he wrote,

00:22:52.327 --> 00:22:56.580 position:63%
"Right before the helium
reached the coldest temperature,

00:22:56.580 --> 00:23:01.580 position:63%
which was 1.8 Kelvin, the
boiling suddenly stopped."

00:23:02.825 --> 00:23:04.320 position:63%
- Um. He noticed.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:05.850 position:63%
- He noticed.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:09.810 position:63%
- So what did Onnes do after
seeing this sudden change?

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:11.070 position:63%
Almost nothing.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:14.373 position:63%
He guessed at an ordinary
explanation and moved on.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:18.270 position:63%
But helium kept dropping clues.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:19.800 position:63%
When they measured its density,

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.970 position:63%
they discovered it had a
curious peak around 2.17 Kelvin.

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:26.100 position:63%
And when they measured the latent heat,

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:27.960 position:63%
they found that around
the same temperature,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.700 position:63%
something happened to the helium,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.640 position:63%
which was perhaps even discontinuous.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.950 position:63%
Yet the biggest smoking
gun of all happened

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:37.860 position:63%
when they measured a property
known as the specific heat,

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.630 position:63%
which is how much energy it takes

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:42.570 position:63%
to heat up one gram by one Kelvin.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:44.310 position:63%
They found that as the
temperature dropped,

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:46.530 position:63%
the specific heat declined

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.050 position:63%
until they approached 2.17 Kelvin,

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:50.493 position:63%
where it suddenly shot up.

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.790 position:63%
This was as clear a sign as any

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:56.223 position:63%
that something was
happening at this point.

00:23:57.090 --> 00:23:58.320 position:63%
- But they knew what this was.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.990 position:63%
This was condensation happening,

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:03.330 position:63%
you know, experimental error.

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:06.097 position:63%
So they had these points,
but they wrote like,

00:24:06.097 --> 00:24:10.680 position:63%
"We felt justified in disregarding
these irregular points",

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:14.310 position:63%
and they only published
the part above 2.61 Kelvin,

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.947 position:63%
which neatly fit the
formula they had for it.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:22.200 position:63%
- Onnes brushed off hint after hint,

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.663 position:63%
even while seeing it
happen with his own eyes.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:27.660 position:63%
One day, Onnes put one container

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:29.700 position:63%
of liquid helium inside another,

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.580 position:63%
and he noticed that below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:36.213 position:63%
if one level started higher,
it always quickly evened out.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.590 position:63%
He even remarked how
strikingly fast it happened.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:42.930 position:63%
He thought it was too
fast for distillation,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:47.100 position:63%
but since he had no better
explanation, he stuck with that.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.320 position:63%
If only he had raised his container,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.630 position:63%
he might have discovered the truth.

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:56.070 position:63%
Here, we've got a polymer
cup. This is our container.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:59.100 position:63%
- So we got the bowl loaded up.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:00.450 position:63%
It's way at the bottom,

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.980 position:63%
and I think what we're going
to try and do is lift it up.

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:04.710 position:63%
- Yeah.
- The colder we get,

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:06.150 position:63%
the better this effect is gonna be,

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:09.810 position:63%
and it's a super subtle
effect at the best of times.

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:13.590 position:63%
We want to get down to about 1.5, 1.55 K.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.390 position:63%
I'm gonna try and raise it
just above the fluid surface.

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:19.620 position:63%
- [Casper] Whoa.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:20.472 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] Can you see that?

00:25:20.472 --> 00:25:21.840 position:63%
- [Casper] Yeah, yeah.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:25.200 position:63%
- Yeah, no,
you see like waves almost.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:26.430 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] Yeah.
- [Casper] Yeah.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:28.923 position:63%
Ah, you can really see a pulse down there.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:34.110 position:63%
- If you look at the bottom,
you can see it's dripping.

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:37.023 position:63%
And if we speed it up, you
can see it keeps dripping.

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:40.863 position:63%
More and more liquid helium
is going to the bottom.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:45.450 position:63%
What's happening is that somehow
the helium is climbing up

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:48.843 position:63%
and over the walls, literally
emptying itself out.

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:52.410 position:63%
This was not distillation.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:54.573 position:63%
Something far stranger was happening.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:25:59.970 position:63%
Onnes's successors grew suspicious

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:03.000 position:63%
of the specific heat
data, so they remeasured

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:06.390 position:63%
and they became convinced
it wasn't an error.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:07.710 position:63%
This was real.

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:10.950 position:63%
Something was happening at 2.17 Kelvin.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:13.470 position:63%
Since the curve resembled
the Greek letter Lambda,

00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:15.510 position:63%
they called this the Lambda point

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:17.640 position:63%
and they suggested that a phase transition

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:19.590 position:63%
must be happening here.

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:22.110 position:63%
But not an ordinary phase transition,

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:23.820 position:63%
this would be a phase transition

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:26.580 position:63%
to a more exotic kind of matter,

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:29.820 position:63%
from a normal liquid to something else.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:30.653 position:63%
But what?

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:33.780 position:63%
Figuring out what's really happening

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:36.810 position:63%
when there are so many different
clues can be confusing.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:38.610 position:63%
If not, outright impossible.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:40.620 position:63%
The same is still true today.

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:44.280 position:63%
Recently, I came across this
story where scientists used AI

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.860 position:63%
to design viruses not found in nature,

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:48.660 position:63%
but depending on where you read the story,

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:50.820 position:63%
you might have a completely
different reaction.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:52.980 position:63%
If you read IFLS first,

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:55.860 position:63%
you might think this is an
exciting scientific breakthrough,

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:59.160 position:63%
AI designing a virus
that can kill bacteria.

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:00.600 position:63%
If you'd seen this first,

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:04.050 position:63%
you'd get a more complete,
albeit complex picture,

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:07.920 position:63%
16 viruses from scratch,
but with possible downsides.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.140 position:63%
And if you saw this New
York Post article first,

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.050 position:63%
you'd most likely focus
on what could go wrong.

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:14.190 position:63%
So three headlines,

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:16.860 position:63%
but three completely
different conclusions.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:19.140 position:63%
Most people just get their
news from one source,

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.960 position:63%
and as a result, their
takeaway is a little bit

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:23.490 position:63%
like the luck of the draw,

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:25.710 position:63%
which outlet did you happen to see first?

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:28.020 position:63%
That's why we use today's
