I'd like to thank Ground News

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:32.220 position:63%
for sponsoring this part of the video,

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.140 position:63%
and now back to what was happening

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:36.810 position:63%
when you keep cooling down helium.

00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:40.260 position:63%
The answer started to take
shape throughout the 1930s.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:43.800 position:63%
- So in 1935, there was
this graduate student

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:47.670 position:63%
called Don Misener, and he
needed to do some experiments

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:50.100 position:63%
for his coursework to get enough credit.

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:53.610 position:63%
And he was aware of everything
that was happening in helium,

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:56.550 position:63%
but he realized that no one
had measured its viscosity.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:29:00.120 position:63%
So he's like, "Oh, that's
weird. I will measure it."

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.820 position:63%
- [Derek] So how do you
measure the viscosity?

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:04.740 position:63%
- There are two ways.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:06.050 position:63%
You have a disc

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:13.280 position:63%
and you try to see what the
resistance of the liquid is.

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:18.447 position:63%
Another way is to have a small capillary,

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:22.533 position:63%
and then you push the liquid through that,

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:26.410 position:63%
and the viscosity you measure

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:32.570 position:63%
between the two methods
should be exactly the same.

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:36.210 position:63%
- Misener chose a variation
of the first method.

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:37.860 position:63%
He submerged a cylinder

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:41.460 position:63%
that was connected through a
torsion fiber in liquid helium.

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.830 position:63%
He then rotated the cylinder and let go,

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:47.490 position:63%
causing the cylinder to swing
back and forth to oscillate.

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:49.290 position:63%
Now, by watching how long it oscillated,

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:51.030 position:63%
he could determine the viscosity.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:52.110 position:63%
The longer it oscillated,

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.090 position:63%
the less friction it must experience,

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:55.950 position:63%
so the lower the viscosity.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.230 position:63%
And what he found was that at 4.2 Kelvin,

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:02.640 position:63%
the viscosity was about 14%
that of air, already tiny.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:04.680 position:63%
Then, as he decreased the temperature,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.110 position:63%
the viscosity slowly increased

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:10.680 position:63%
until at the transition
point, it suddenly plummeted,

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:14.610 position:63%
not to zero, but to about 5% that of air.

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.440 position:63%
This caught the attention of Jack Allen,

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:19.710 position:63%
a 27-year-old physicist
who'd just come to Cambridge.

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:21.750 position:63%
Allen had inherited a helium liquefier

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.520 position:63%
from someone who could no longer use it,

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.170 position:63%
and now he resolved to use it

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.840 position:63%
to unravel the mysteries of helium.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:29.580 position:63%
Of course, he could use some help,

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.460 position:63%
and Misener seemed like
the perfect PhD student,

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.590 position:63%
so Allen convinced him, and together,

00:30:34.590 --> 00:30:37.530 position:63%
they continued studying
helium's viscosity.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:40.590 position:63%
Only now they turn to the
second method of studying it,

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:42.720 position:63%
which works something like this.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:45.570 position:63%
I've got a tube filled with
two layers of glycerol.

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:47.970 position:63%
Now, if I let it flow,
what's gonna happen?

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:51.180 position:63%
Well, you'd expect the layers
to drop, which they do,

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.040 position:63%
but not in a straightforward way.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.630 position:63%
It doesn't maintain its shape.

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:57.450 position:63%
Instead, it stretches out in the middle.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.610 position:63%
What's happening is that if you zoom in,

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.010 position:63%
you'd find that the molecules
near the wall get stuck,

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:03.360 position:63%
they experience friction,

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.190 position:63%
and then the molecules sliding past them

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:07.380 position:63%
experience friction with those molecules.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.623 position:63%
The internal friction is
basically what viscosity is.

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:13.620 position:63%
In general, if you make
the channel smaller,

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:15.330 position:63%
the flow rate should decrease,

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:18.210 position:63%
and if you push on the
liquid, it should increase.

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:20.910 position:63%
Now, by carefully keeping
track of how things change

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:22.680 position:63%
when you alter these parameters,

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:24.390 position:63%
you can determine the viscosity.

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:26.160 position:63%
In November of 1937,

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.380 position:63%
Allen and Misener did a
version of this experiment

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.870 position:63%
with liquid helium at around 1 Kelvin.

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:33.150 position:63%
They let it flow through
one wide capillary

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:36.180 position:63%
and one narrow one, and they
found that the flow speed

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:38.250 position:63%
through both was almost the same,

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:40.620 position:63%
despite the narrow one
having a surface area

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:42.720 position:63%
a thousand times as small.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.600 position:63%
Even weirder was that
the speed hardly changed

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.100 position:63%
when the pressure increased.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:49.530 position:63%
They concluded that any
known formula cannot,

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.990 position:63%
from our data, give a
value of the viscosity,

00:31:51.990 --> 00:31:53.700 position:63%
which would have much meaning.

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:58.140 position:63%
As far as they could tell, the
viscosity was non-existent.

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:00.810 position:63%
- They know they've got gold
on their hands here, right?

00:32:00.810 --> 00:32:05.280 position:63%
They know this is like
a bombshell of a result.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.290 position:63%
- [Derek] So they spent the
next three weeks making sure

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.090 position:63%
they hadn't made any mistakes.

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:10.500 position:63%
They repeated all the experiments

00:32:10.500 --> 00:32:12.420 position:63%
and did all the other
measurements they needed,

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:13.593 position:63%
ready to write it up.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:16.500 position:63%
- But then on the last day,

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:19.380 position:63%
one of Kapitsa's old students walks in

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:20.913 position:63%
with a letter from Kapitsa.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:22.950 position:63%
- Who's Kapitsa?

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.590 position:63%
Well, remember that Allen
had inherited that liquefier

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:27.660 position:63%
from someone who couldn't use it anymore?

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:31.110 position:63%
Well, that was Soviet
physicist Pyotr Kapitsa.

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.933 position:63%
He built the liquefier and
ran the lab before Allen.

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.850 position:63%
But then during a trip back
home in the summer of 1934,

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.193 position:63%
Stalin had his passport confiscated.

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:44.700 position:63%
He was to work and stay
in the Soviet Union,

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:45.930 position:63%
where for the past few years,

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:48.030 position:63%
Kapitsa had been rebuilding his lab,

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:51.570 position:63%
telling almost no one exactly
what he was working on.

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:54.243 position:63%
But Allen and Misener
were about to find out.

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:59.550 position:63%
- He had done the same sort of experiment,

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:02.370 position:63%
found the viscosity was vanishingly small,

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.460 position:63%
and send a note to the editor of Nature

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.400 position:63%
that he would be very grateful
if they could publish it

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:10.500 position:63%
as soon as possible.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:13.170 position:63%
Don't bother to send the
proofs to me here to Moscow.

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:15.690 position:63%
It takes too much time.

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.864 position:63%
He had just scooped his old lab.

00:33:17.864 --> 00:33:20.730 position:63%
- He knew what he had
and he raced to publish.

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:23.310 position:63%
Allen and Misener were freaking out,

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:24.540 position:63%
but over the next four days,

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:27.693 position:63%
they wrote up all their results
and also sent it to Nature.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:31.140 position:63%
On the 8th of January, 1938,

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:33.930 position:63%
the two papers appeared side by side,

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.333 position:63%
reporting the vanishingly small viscosity,

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:39.330 position:63%
perhaps even zero.

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:41.970 position:63%
And then in analogy to superconductors,

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:44.703 position:63%
Kapitsa called it a superfluid.

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:48.450 position:63%
This was the breakthrough
everyone had been waiting for

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:50.430 position:63%
because it finally began to explain

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:53.310 position:63%
some of the strange
things they'd been seeing.

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:57.120 position:63%
Above 2.17 Kelvin, helium
is just a normal fluid

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.520 position:63%
and it has internal friction or viscosity,

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:01.650 position:63%
so when it flows through tiny cracks,

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:04.140 position:63%
part of it gets stuck to the walls.

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:05.670 position:63%
Now, in the case of the bucket,

00:34:05.670 --> 00:34:07.920 position:63%
the bottom is made of a polymer aerogel,

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:10.140 position:63%
which is filled with microscopic channels

00:34:10.140 --> 00:34:12.510 position:63%
up to tens of nanometers across.

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:15.480 position:63%
That's so small that the
helium gets fully clamped

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:18.120 position:63%
to the walls, it can't flow through.

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:21.690 position:63%
But as you cool down below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:24.120 position:63%
the helium transforms,

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:26.910 position:63%
and now it can suddenly
pass through tiny cracks

00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:30.543 position:63%
with no friction, so it leaks
straight through the bottom.

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:36.780 position:63%
It also explained what was
happening with the little cup.

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:37.800 position:63%
How?

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:39.960 position:63%
Well, remember those van der Waals forces

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.600 position:63%
that cause every atom
to attract every other?

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:45.480 position:63%
Well, between helium
atoms, they're very weak,

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:47.250 position:63%
but the atoms in the walls of the cup

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:49.500 position:63%
have many more electrons
in their outer shell,

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:52.530 position:63%
so the charge imbalances become larger.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:53.363 position:63%
And as a result,

00:34:53.363 --> 00:34:56.160 position:63%
the helium atoms are much more
strongly drawn to the wall

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:57.960 position:63%
than to each other.

00:34:57.960 --> 00:34:59.790 position:63%
So they climb up the wall

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:02.250 position:63%
and within seconds wet the entire surface

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:04.803 position:63%
with a layer that's around
30 nanometers thick.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:08.340 position:63%
And because the bottom of this thin sheet

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:12.540 position:63%
is below the liquid layer,
it now acts as a siphon.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.100 position:63%
Normal fluid can't flow through

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:17.763 position:63%
because it experiences
friction, but superfluid can,

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:21.210 position:63%
so it flows up and over the walls

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:23.283 position:63%
until it drips out the bottom.

00:35:23.283 --> 00:35:26.283 position:63%
(suspenseful music)

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:34.110 position:63%
But for all the idea of
a superfluid explained,

00:35:34.110 --> 00:35:37.110 position:63%
it also raised a much
more fundamental question.

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.450 position:63%
How do you get a superfluid?

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.220 position:63%
- Like if it is actually zero,

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.440 position:63%
I want you to think
about how crazy that is.

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:45.000 position:63%
Right, because if we think back to,

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.370 position:63%
okay, what is this viscosity?

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:49.800 position:63%
It's internal friction
between the molecules.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.680 position:63%
So if you have actually zero viscosity,

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:56.700 position:63%
it means that you have like
a billion, billion particles

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:58.320 position:63%
all traveling through this pipe

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:02.100 position:63%
and not a single one is
bouncing off of anything

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:04.680 position:63%
and losing some energy or
bouncing into each other

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:05.940 position:63%
and losing energy.

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:08.440 position:63%
They all must flow perfectly

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:12.180 position:63%
and somehow not lose a
single bit of energy.

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:15.450 position:63%
It seems preposterous. (chuckles)

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:17.400 position:63%
- [Derek] Within two months
of the nature papers,

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:19.950 position:63%
Fritz London proposed
a possible mechanism.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.100 position:63%
There was this forgotten
paper from 13 years earlier.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.230 position:63%
It predicted that if you
just kept cooling down

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:26.310 position:63%
a certain type of particle,

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:29.400 position:63%
then the atoms would fall
into an exotic state.

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:31.380 position:63%
And the author of that forgotten paper was

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:34.110 position:63%
none other than Albert Einstein.

00:36:34.110 --> 00:36:36.090 position:63%
To understand what Einstein had predicted,

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:38.250 position:63%
consider this plane of particles.

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:39.630 position:63%
They're not interacting

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:42.120 position:63%
and they're free to move
around in every direction

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:45.000 position:63%
with different velocities and
different kinetic energies.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:46.230 position:63%
Now at room temperature,

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:49.500 position:63%
you can just think of
them as point particles,

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.540 position:63%
but if you start cooling them down,

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:53.190 position:63%
what Einstein realized is that,

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:55.170 position:63%
well, this is no longer the case

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:57.900 position:63%
because now quantum theory
starts to play a role.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:36:58.980 position:63%
And according to that theory,

00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:00.270 position:63%
particles aren't just particles,

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:02.790 position:63%
they also have a wave-like character.

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:04.560 position:63%
- So-called wave particle duality,

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:08.100 position:63%
the quantum wave function
tells you the likelihood

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:08.940 position:63%
of where it would be found

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:11.460 position:63%
if you were to try to
measure its location.

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:13.650 position:63%
- [Derek] The important thing
here is that the wavelength

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:16.620 position:63%
of these waves or how
spread out they are is equal

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:18.660 position:63%
to Planck's constant over their momentum

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:20.640 position:63%
or mass times velocity.

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:22.770 position:63%
- [David] So if you're
cooling these particles down,

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:25.560 position:63%
they on average have less and
less momentum per particle,

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:30.150 position:63%
their quantum wavelength or
the de Broglie wavelength gets longer.

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:33.870 position:63%
And so eventually the sort
of quantum state associated

00:37:33.870 --> 00:37:36.900 position:63%
with each of those seemingly
independent particles starts

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:38.940 position:63%
to smoosh out and overlap.

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:42.483 position:63%
This distinguishability in
space starts to melt away.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:45.060 position:63%
- What happens next depends

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:47.790 position:63%
on which kind of particle
you're cooling down.

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:50.550 position:63%
Every particle in the
universe is one of two kinds.

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:53.280 position:63%
It's either a boson or a fermion.

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:55.830 position:63%
Fermions are most of the
particles you're familiar with,

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:57.840 position:63%
like protons, neutrons and electrons,

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:00.570 position:63%
and bosons are particles like photons.

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:03.030 position:63%
You can see the difference
between bosons and fermions

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:06.600 position:63%
by looking at two
indistinguishable particles.

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:10.080 position:63%
Now, one particle just
has its own wave function,

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:12.030 position:63%
but if you take two of them together,

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:13.980 position:63%
something special happens.

00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:15.510 position:63%
They get a combined wave function

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:17.360 position:63%
that describes both of them together.

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.900 position:63%
In the case of bosons, if you
could see that wave function,

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:24.270 position:63%
you'd see it's the same on both sides,

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:25.863 position:63%
that is it's symmetric.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:28.923 position:63%
But if those particles are fermions,

00:38:28.923 --> 00:38:31.290 position:63%
then their combined wave
function is the opposite

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:32.430 position:63%
on both sides.

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:34.023 position:63%
It's anti-symmetric.

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:36.960 position:63%
Now I want you to think
about what this means

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:39.900 position:63%
if you try to move those
particles together.

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:41.010 position:63%
In the case of the bosons,

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:43.500 position:63%
the two sides of the wave function add up

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:46.950 position:63%
and you just get two bosons
in the same place or state.

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:49.440 position:63%
But now try doing the same for fermions.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.230 position:63%
You get one peak to
overlap with one valley

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:54.120 position:63%
and they'd cancel out.

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:55.980 position:63%
But that would suggest
that all of a sudden

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:59.460 position:63%
there's a 0% probability of
finding the particles anywhere,

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:01.050 position:63%
which doesn't make any sense.

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:02.280 position:63%
They have to be somewhere.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:04.380 position:63%
So what this is really telling us

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:06.480 position:63%
is that two indistinguishable fermions

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:09.570 position:63%
can never occupy the same quantum state.

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:12.330 position:63%
This is known as Pauli's
exclusion principle,

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:14.670 position:63%
and it's why particles
like electrons can never be

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:16.830 position:63%
in exactly the same state.

00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:19.020 position:63%
If the particles you're
cooling down are fermions,

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:21.990 position:63%
then they start filling up the
energy states like a ladder

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.630 position:63%
from lowest to the higher states,

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:27.270 position:63%
with only one fermion per quantum state.

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:29.730 position:63%
But bosons don't have this restriction.

00:39:29.730 --> 00:39:32.280 position:63%
They're very happy to share
the same quantum state.

00:39:33.120 --> 00:39:34.590 position:63%
When you keep cooling them down,

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:36.840 position:63%
more and more of them
start to occupy lower

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:38.373 position:63%
and lower energy levels.

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:41.520 position:63%
And Einstein realized there comes a point

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.610 position:63%
where a macroscopic
portion suddenly condenses

00:39:44.610 --> 00:39:48.090 position:63%
into the lowest energy
state or ground state,

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.720 position:63%
which in this case is just
when they're completely still.

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:55.260 position:63%
- These little particles
could actually act in a way

00:39:55.260 --> 00:39:56.850 position:63%
where they would sort of lose,

00:39:56.850 --> 00:39:59.370 position:63%
so to speak, their individuality,

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:02.973 position:63%
that they could act as a single
coherent state of matter,

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:07.800 position:63%
a whole new state of
matter that had no analog.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:10.500 position:63%
- This became known as a
Bose-Einstein Condensate

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:13.023 position:63%
after Bose whose work
Einstein was building on.

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.800 position:63%
But Einstein doubted this
could happen in real life.

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:18.600 position:63%
He wrote, "The theory is pretty,

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:20.907 position:63%
but is there also some truth to it?"

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:24.930 position:63%
13 years later, London
believed there might be

00:40:24.930 --> 00:40:25.763 position:63%
and that perhaps

00:40:25.763 --> 00:40:29.220 position:63%
superfluid helium was a
Bose-Einstein Condensate.

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:31.200 position:63%
At first, that might seem impossible

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.610 position:63%
because helium is made
up of protons, neutrons

00:40:34.503 --> 00:40:36.570 position:63%
and electrons, and every
one of those is a fermion.

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:39.570 position:63%
So you'd expect helium
to be a fermion too.

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:42.990 position:63%
And remember, fermions
can't share the same state,

00:40:42.990 --> 00:40:45.777 position:63%
but helium is not a fermion.

00:40:46.628 --> 00:40:49.740 position:63%
Bosons and fermions differ
in one other fundamental way,

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:52.470 position:63%
their intrinsic angular momentum or spin.

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:55.200 position:63%
For bosons, this is
always an integer number,

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:57.750 position:63%
zero, one, two, three, and so on.

00:40:57.750 --> 00:41:01.710 position:63%
But fermions always have a
half integer spin, like a half.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:04.710 position:63%
So what happens if you have
multiple fermions together,

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:06.210 position:63%
say in one atom?

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:08.190 position:63%
Well, now you have to
add their spins together

00:41:08.190 --> 00:41:10.650 position:63%
to get an overall spin for the atom,

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:14.490 position:63%
and then use that to see whether
it's a boson or a fermion.

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:18.120 position:63%
In the case of helium, which
has six half spin particles,

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:20.130 position:63%
then no matter how you add them up,

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:23.040 position:63%
you always end up with an integer number.

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:26.373 position:63%
As a result, helium
atoms behave as bosons.

00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:30.270 position:63%
And as you cool them
down below 2.17 Kelvin,

00:41:30.270 --> 00:41:32.730 position:63%
a macroscopic fraction
of the atoms condense

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:34.980 position:63%
into the lowest energy state.

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:37.380 position:63%
They lose their individuality

00:41:37.380 --> 00:41:42.380 position:63%
and about a trillion trillion
atoms all behave as one.

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.430 position:63%
Normally, you think of wave functions

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:45.900 position:63%
as these teeny tiny things,

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:49.590 position:63%
but here it's literally
the size of the container.

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:51.420 position:63%
It's as if after the transition,

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:54.790 position:63%
the helium has turned into one big atom

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:58.413 position:63%
with all these atoms behaving as one.

00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:04.450 position:63%
- This is quantum mechanics
with your own eyes.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:08.430 position:63%
- And it makes you look at
matter in a different way.

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:11.880 position:63%
It allows us to explore
the boundary of quantum

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:14.430 position:63%
and regular matter.

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:16.590 position:63%
So it seems like we've solved it.

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:19.140 position:63%
The atoms are in the lowest energy state.

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.930 position:63%
They've lost their
individuality and act as one,

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:23.040 position:63%
so they can't scatter

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.653 position:63%
and they flow with zero internal friction.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.960 position:63%
That's how I first understood superfluids

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:33.690 position:63%
and how I'd explain it to
anyone who wanted to know.

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:36.240 position:63%
I mean, it made sense and we were all happy.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:39.150 position:63%
The only problem is that it's wrong.

00:42:39.150 --> 00:42:41.910 position:63%
I mean, the first issue is that in a BEC,

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:43.590 position:63%
all the atoms occupy the state

00:42:43.590 --> 00:42:45.540 position:63%
where the particles are at rest,

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:48.540 position:63%
but in a superfluid, well, they flow.

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:50.460 position:63%
Maybe that issue could be excused

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:53.130 position:63%
because you could imagine
moving along with the superfluid

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:56.340 position:63%
and finding a frame in which
it effectively stands still,

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:58.380 position:63%
but there's an even bigger problem,

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:01.980 position:63%
which is that in a non-interacting
Bose-Einstein condensate,

00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:05.700 position:63%
even the tiniest push can
excite any of the atoms.

00:43:05.700 --> 00:43:07.440 position:63%
So if you'd try to let it flow,

00:43:07.440 --> 00:43:09.720 position:63%
the smallest disturbance would knock atoms

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.040 position:63%
out of the ground state.

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:13.980 position:63%
They would scatter and the
flow would lose energy.

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:17.250 position:63%
So clearly a BEC alone isn't enough.

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.743 position:63%
You need something else, but what?

00:43:20.850 --> 00:43:22.920 position:63%
Fortunately, Kapitsa knew exactly

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.660 position:63%
who could solve the problem.

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:27.930 position:63%
Unfortunately, he had been
locked inside Stalin's prison

00:43:27.930 --> 00:43:29.460 position:63%
for the last year.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:32.640 position:63%
- This was right around the
time of Stalin's great terror

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:36.180 position:63%
where he was executing or imprisoning

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:37.530 position:63%
all the enemies of state,

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:40.470 position:63%
whether they were real or imaginary.

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:41.880 position:63%
- [Derek] But in the case of Lev Landau,

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:44.010 position:63%
the physicist Kapitsa was thinking about,

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:46.890 position:63%
it seems like there was some truth to it.

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:49.260 position:63%
Landau was known as a genius theorist

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:52.200 position:63%
who cared deeply about
elevating Soviet physics.

00:43:52.200 --> 00:43:53.730 position:63%
He was the head of the physics department

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:54.690 position:63%
and personally trained

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:57.570 position:63%
some of the best
physicists of the century.

00:43:57.570 --> 00:43:59.190 position:63%
But around the mid 1930s,

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:02.040 position:63%
he became disillusioned
with Stalin's regime.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:03.630 position:63%
He reportedly told one colleague

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:06.930 position:63%
that Soviet officials
were "born subhuman."

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:09.780 position:63%
And according to KGB Files, he was accused

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:12.393 position:63%
of heading a counter-revolutionary
organization.

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:16.759 position:63%
Landau was arrested on
the 28th of April 1938

00:44:16.759 --> 00:44:18.409 position:63%
and taken to the Lubyanka Prison.

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.430 position:63%
The first day he was forced
to stand for seven hours,

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:27.720 position:63%
followed by six days of
12-hour persuasion sessions.

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:28.650 position:63%
After two months,

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:31.440 position:63%
Landau broke and wrote
a six-page confession.

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:33.510 position:63%
It reads, "We came to the conclusion

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:34.950 position:63%
that the party had degenerated

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:37.020 position:63%
and that the Soviet
government no longer acted

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:38.340 position:63%
in the interests of workers,

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:41.460 position:63%
but in the interests of
a small ruling group.

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:43.830 position:63%
The interests of the country
demanded the overthrow

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:45.780 position:63%
of the existing government."

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:48.540 position:63%
- That's a pretty,
pretty strong confession.

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:49.560 position:63%
- Um hm!

00:44:49.560 --> 00:44:52.500 position:63%
This confession at a time where
people were being executed

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:56.100 position:63%
for no reason should have
meant the end for Landau.

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:59.250 position:63%
But fortunately for him, he
had someone defending him.

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:00.615 position:63%
The morning Landau got arrested,

00:45:00.615 --> 00:45:03.390 position:63%
Kapitsa immediately wrote to Stalin.

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:05.970 position:63%
His character, to put it simply, is nasty.

00:45:05.970 --> 00:45:07.830 position:63%
He is a bully and a brawler,

00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:09.210 position:63%
but I find it very hard to believe

00:45:09.210 --> 00:45:12.030 position:63%
that Landau was capable
of anything dishonest.

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:13.050 position:63%
Landau is young

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:16.020 position:63%
and he still has much to
accomplish in science.

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:17.850 position:63%
No one else can write about all of this,

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:19.450 position:63%
which is why I'm writing to you.

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:22.950 position:63%
Now, Stalin never replied.

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:24.060 position:63%
So a year later,

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:26.760 position:63%
Kapitsa wrote to one of
Stalin's closest allies.

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:29.220 position:63%
He told him how he had
discovered superfluidity

00:45:29.220 --> 00:45:32.193 position:63%
and that no theorist other
than Landau could explain it.

00:45:33.330 --> 00:45:36.180 position:63%
Three weeks later, Kapitsa
was summoned to the prison.

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:38.820 position:63%
As he recalled, "I was
summoned at 1:00 AM.

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:42.360 position:63%
They escorted me to a big
office occupied by two men.

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:44.550 position:63%
Do you understand for
whom you are pleading?

00:45:44.550 --> 00:45:46.020 position:63%
He's a most dangerous criminal,

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:47.907 position:63%
a spy who confessed to everything."

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:50.640 position:63%
We talked till four in the morning.

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:53.310 position:63%
Towards the end, one of them
said, "All right, Kapitsa,

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:55.050 position:63%
if you pledge your word for Landau,

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:56.970 position:63%
then give us a written guarantee.

00:45:56.970 --> 00:46:00.690 position:63%
If anything happens, you
will be held responsible."

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:03.570 position:63%
Kapitsa wrote the letter
and two days later,

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:05.490 position:63%
Landau turned up at the institute,

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:08.853 position:63%
ready to get back to what he
loved doing most, physics.

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:13.380 position:63%
Kapitsa had put his neck on the line.

00:46:13.380 --> 00:46:16.740 position:63%
Now it was up to Landau to
prove he was up to the task,

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:20.550 position:63%
and superfluid helium had
only gotten more interesting.

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:24.510 position:63%
Back in January 1938, just
days after the Nature papers,

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:27.870 position:63%
Allen was studying how
superfluid helium conducts heat.

00:46:27.870 --> 00:46:30.150 position:63%
To do this, he packed some Emery powder,

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:33.930 position:63%
basically fine sand, in a
glass bulb with an open bottom.

00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:37.500 position:63%
To see what was happening, he
shone his pocket torch at it.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:38.880 position:63%
And according to him,

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:41.580 position:63%
as the Emery powder tube came into view,

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:44.490 position:63%
helium spurted out the top of the tube.

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:46.020 position:63%
It continued to spurt as long

00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:48.660 position:63%
as the light shone on
the powder and ceased

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:50.970 position:63%
when a card cut off the light.

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:53.700 position:63%
Allen later showed this
fountain with no moving parts

00:46:53.700 --> 00:46:55.170 position:63%
to the Royal Society,

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:57.330 position:63%
during which famous writer H.G. Wells

00:46:57.330 --> 00:47:00.030 position:63%
crawled under the table
looking for a hidden pump,

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:00.930 position:63%
but found nothing.

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:04.530 position:63%
- Yeah, so we had this
kind of specialist made.

00:47:04.530 --> 00:47:05.790 position:63%
It's a single piece of glass.

00:47:05.790 --> 00:47:07.350 position:63%
There are no seams in it whatsoever.

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:09.660 position:63%
The filtered disc is fused in,

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:12.030 position:63%
and then it's just filled
with carbon stones,

00:47:12.030 --> 00:47:13.650 position:63%
and we just hit it with a bright light

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:15.720 position:63%
that heats it up, drives
the fountain flow.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:18.660 position:63%
- [Casper] Amazing. Fingers crossed.

00:47:18.660 --> 00:47:20.250 position:63%
- You guys ready?

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:23.103 position:63%
Three, two, one. Torch it.

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.173 position:63%
Whoa! Yeah.

00:47:32.028 --> 00:47:33.945 position:63%
Let's go. That is huge.

00:47:34.929 --> 00:47:36.596 position:63%
What? That's absurd.

00:47:37.962 --> 00:47:41.462 position:63%
- That's awesome. 
- Woo. Let's go.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:47.700 position:63%
- [Derek] So what was happening?

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:50.223 position:63%
How could light cause
the helium to shoot out?

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:56.340 position:63%
- Any theory now had to
take into consideration

00:47:56.340 --> 00:47:57.903 position:63%
all these phenomena.

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:04.040 position:63%
So for theoreticians
was a really heavy task.

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:07.620 position:63%
- [Derek] Landau spent the
next eight years working on it.

00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:09.570 position:63%
He started by considering
a superfluid flowing

00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:13.890 position:63%
at absolute zero, and he realized
what London had left out,

00:48:13.890 --> 00:48:16.110 position:63%
the forces between atoms.

00:48:16.110 --> 00:48:17.670 position:63%
In a Bose-Einstein condensate,

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:19.890 position:63%
there are no forces between particles,

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:22.590 position:63%
and as a result, a push
can knock a single atom

00:48:22.590 --> 00:48:26.040 position:63%
out of the lowest energy state,
resulting in easy friction.

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:28.800 position:63%
But in liquid helium,
this is not the case.

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:31.410 position:63%
The atoms interact, so if you push on one,

00:48:31.410 --> 00:48:33.360 position:63%
you're pushing on a bunch of them.

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:35.640 position:63%
Landau realized that this rigidity meant

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:37.830 position:63%
that the only way you
can create excitations

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:40.470 position:63%
in a superfluid and
lose energy in the flow

00:48:40.470 --> 00:48:43.440 position:63%
is through the collective
motion of the atoms.

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:47.430 position:63%
He argued that these excitations
come in two main flavors.

00:48:47.430 --> 00:48:49.320 position:63%
The first is that if you push hard enough,

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:51.150 position:63%
you get the atoms to bump into each other

00:48:51.150 --> 00:48:53.310 position:63%
and create ripples throughout the fluid,

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:54.930 position:63%
basically sound waves,

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:58.020 position:63%
just a quantized version
of them called phonons.

00:48:58.020 --> 00:49:00.600 position:63%
Similar to how photons
are particles of light,

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:03.600 position:63%
these are particles of sound.

00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:06.780 position:63%
The second kind of excitation
is something known as a roton,

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:10.350 position:63%
because originally people
thought of them as tiny whirls,

00:49:10.350 --> 00:49:13.110 position:63%
but now they're seeing
more like a patch of atoms

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:15.510 position:63%
that suddenly orders themselves
into a crystal lattice

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:17.280 position:63%
just for a split second.

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:20.640 position:63%
You can think of them almost
as tiny ghosts of a solid.

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:21.990 position:63%
And just like a phonon

00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:25.440 position:63%
creating a roton costs
both energy and momentum.

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:27.077 position:63%
So Landau argued that these phonons

00:49:27.077 --> 00:49:29.370 position:63%
and rotons were the only way

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:32.280 position:63%
that the flow could lose energy.

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:34.950 position:63%
- And you're like, okay,
well, it's quite easy,

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:36.180 position:63%
you know, to make a sound wave.

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:38.610 position:63%
Like all I have to do
is knock on it, right?

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:41.220 position:63%
That in terms of like
how much energy it takes

00:49:41.220 --> 00:49:44.190 position:63%
to create a phonon, it
can be very little energy.

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:46.350 position:63%
It can be as little energy as you want,

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:48.990 position:63%
but you don't just have
to conserve energy.

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:50.700 position:63%
You also have to conserve momentum.

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:52.350 position:63%
So when you push on it

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:54.630 position:63%
or when the liquid bumps into something,

00:49:54.630 --> 00:49:57.180 position:63%
it both needs to satisfy
conservation of energy

00:49:57.180 --> 00:49:59.370 position:63%
and conservation of momentum.

00:49:59.370 --> 00:50:04.140 position:63%
And there is a very nice
relationship that tells you exactly

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:06.390 position:63%
how the momentum of a phonon corresponds

00:50:06.390 --> 00:50:08.160 position:63%
to the energy of a phonon,

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:12.600 position:63%
and it's connected with the
speed of sound in a material.

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:15.510 position:63%
And so the only way you can
satisfy both constraints is

00:50:15.510 --> 00:50:19.260 position:63%
if the flow is going at
least the speed of sound,

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:21.600 position:63%
which in helium two is very fast.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.260 position:63%
It's about, I think, 220
or 240 meters per second.

00:50:25.260 --> 00:50:29.280 position:63%
Rotons, he realizes, can get
created a little bit easier.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:32.370 position:63%
I think the modern value is
about 60 meters per second,

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:33.990 position:63%
but that's still a lot.

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:37.020 position:63%
- In Landau's theory, below
about 60 meters per second,

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:40.290 position:63%
the flow can't create a
single phonon or roton.

00:50:40.290 --> 00:50:42.060 position:63%
The atoms all stay in the ground state

00:50:42.060 --> 00:50:43.830 position:63%
and no energy is lost.

00:50:43.830 --> 00:50:46.023 position:63%
So it's not that the
friction is vanishingly small

00:50:46.023 --> 00:50:47.250 position:63%
in a superfluid.

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:50.100 position:63%
Landau proved that it's strictly zero.

00:50:50.100 --> 00:50:52.110 position:63%
Friction is forbidden.

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:55.260 position:63%
In practice, other excitations
are created at lower speeds

00:50:55.260 --> 00:50:57.030 position:63%
and therefore the actual flow rate

00:50:57.030 --> 00:51:00.510 position:63%
at which superfluidity
breaks down is lower.

00:51:00.510 --> 00:51:02.730 position:63%
But if the friction is actually zero,

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:04.500 position:63%
that implies something else.

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:07.440 position:63%
You should be able to get a
superfluid flowing in a loop,

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:08.490 position:63%
and once it's going,

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:12.330 position:63%
it should just keep going like
a perpetual motion machine.

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:15.720 position:63%
In 1964, two physicists
at Yale tested this.

00:51:15.720 --> 00:51:17.700 position:63%
They got superfluid helium circling

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:20.580 position:63%
in a stack of thin rings and waited.

00:51:20.580 --> 00:51:22.560 position:63%
And what they found was that no matter

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:26.130 position:63%
whether they waited five
minutes or more than 12 hours,

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:29.370 position:63%
the flow was just as strong.

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:32.190 position:63%
- But then, you know,
if the friction is zero,

00:51:32.190 --> 00:51:33.990 position:63%
how is it possible that Misener,

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:36.600 position:63%
you know, when he did his
like torsion experiment,

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:37.890 position:63%
he didn't measure zero.

00:51:37.890 --> 00:51:40.560 position:63%
- The liquid wasn't just
passing over liquid,

00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:42.559 position:63%
like you had like a cylinder
of something in there?

00:51:42.559 --> 00:51:43.710 position:63%
- Yep. Yep.

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:45.690 position:63%
- I don't know. Is it
coming from the wire?

00:51:45.690 --> 00:51:47.270 position:63%
Is it coming from the cylinder?

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:48.150 position:63%
- No.

00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:49.980 position:63%
- The different materials?
What is it coming from?

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:51.540 position:63%
- Your instinct

00:51:51.540 --> 00:51:54.210 position:63%
is that Misener must have
done something wrong.

00:51:54.210 --> 00:51:55.770 position:63%
He didn't.

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:59.010 position:63%
Both experiments are true.
How can both be true?

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:00.510 position:63%
- You're measuring different things.

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:03.990 position:63%
I don't know, but how are you
measuring different things?

00:52:03.990 --> 00:52:06.930 position:63%
This is where the second part
of Landau's work comes in.

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.710 position:63%
So far, Landau had only looked
at a fluid at absolute zero,

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:14.070 position:63%
but every real experiment
happened at higher temperatures.

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:15.540 position:63%
So next he considered

00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:18.720 position:63%
what if it flows at
some finite temperature?

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:21.420 position:63%
To do this, he built on the work of Tisza,

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:24.150 position:63%
who suggested that
helium below 2.17 Kelvin

00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:26.940 position:63%
acts as a mixture of two fluids.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:30.150 position:63%
One is the superfluid. It's
effectively at absolute zero.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:33.090 position:63%
It carries no heat and
flows without friction.

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:36.210 position:63%
You can think of this
as the background fluid.

00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:38.070 position:63%
And the second is the normal fluid,

00:52:38.070 --> 00:52:40.590 position:63%
which is made up of
the phonons and rotons.

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:43.230 position:63%
It carries all the heat and has viscosity.

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:46.740 position:63%
At absolute zero, the
whole fluid is superfluid,

00:52:46.740 --> 00:52:47.910 position:63%
but as it heats up,

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:50.430 position:63%
part of the superfluid
turns into normal fluid.

00:52:50.430 --> 00:52:52.800 position:63%
The superfluid fraction decreases

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:54.780 position:63%
and the normal fraction increases

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:59.073 position:63%
until at 2.17 Kelvin, the
superfluid component is zero.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:02.827 position:63%
- Now, if you were to look at
one atom in the fluid and say,

00:53:02.827 --> 00:53:05.070 position:63%
"Is that superfluid or normal fluid?"

00:53:05.070 --> 00:53:06.240 position:63%
Well, it's hard to tell

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:09.810 position:63%
because the superfluid atoms
are constantly exchanging

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:11.050 position:63%
energy with the normal fluid atoms.

00:53:11.050 --> 00:53:12.570 position:63%
- Sure.
- So any one atom

00:53:12.570 --> 00:53:16.200 position:63%
is constantly bouncing up and
down between these two modes.

00:53:16.200 --> 00:53:19.440 position:63%
- Now, only the superfluid can
flow through tiny channels.

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:21.420 position:63%
So when Allen and Misener pushed helium

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:22.530 position:63%
through their capillaries,

00:53:22.530 --> 00:53:24.690 position:63%
they only measured the superfluid part

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:26.700 position:63%
and they found a zero viscosity.

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:29.580 position:63%
But Misener's torsion
experiment sat in a mixture

00:53:29.580 --> 00:53:30.750 position:63%
of both fluids.

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:34.500 position:63%
So the cylinder felt the
viscosity from the normal fluid.

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:36.330 position:63%
Misener hadn't made a mistake,

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:39.210 position:63%
they were just measuring different things.

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:40.800 position:63%
And once you know what you're looking for,

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.200 position:63%
you can also physically see the effect.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:46.710 position:63%
Just below the transition
temperature, say at 2.15 Kelvin,

00:53:46.710 --> 00:53:49.860 position:63%
only around 13% of the
helium is superfluid.

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:52.860 position:63%
So only that 13% can
leak through the bottom

00:53:52.860 --> 00:53:54.540 position:63%
and you get a drip.

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:56.760 position:63%
But if you cool it down to 1.5 Kelvin,

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:59.790 position:63%
well now around 89% is superfluid.

00:53:59.790 --> 00:54:01.650 position:63%
That's almost seven times as much helium

00:54:01.650 --> 00:54:05.043 position:63%
that can flow through, so it
just pours out the bottom.

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:09.093 position:63%
It even explains how light
can power a fountain.

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:11.670 position:63%
When the light hits the rocks,

00:54:11.670 --> 00:54:13.650 position:63%
the rocks absorb some of the photons

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:15.270 position:63%
and heat up a little bit.

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:16.850 position:63%
This causes part of the superfluid

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:19.110 position:63%
to turn into normal fluid,

00:54:19.110 --> 00:54:22.350 position:63%
but now the superfluid density
is lower inside the fountain,

00:54:22.350 --> 00:54:25.200 position:63%
so more superfluid immediately rushes in

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:27.630 position:63%
through the bottom to
restore the proportions.

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:30.420 position:63%
That bottom, however, is just
like the bottom of the bucket.

00:54:30.420 --> 00:54:32.550 position:63%
It only allows superfluid through.

00:54:32.550 --> 00:54:35.340 position:63%
So the normal fluid can't go out that way.

00:54:35.340 --> 00:54:39.360 position:63%
The only way the excess
helium can go is up and out.

00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:41.023 position:63%
And so you end up with this,

00:54:41.023 --> 00:54:43.620 position:63%
a fountain powered solely by light.

00:54:43.620 --> 00:54:45.210 position:63%
The heat from the light gets turned

00:54:45.210 --> 00:54:47.853 position:63%
into the mechanical energy
of the spouting helium.

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:52.140 position:63%
But the two fluids do
more than make fountains.

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:55.020 position:63%
They change how helium transfers heat.

00:54:55.020 --> 00:54:57.660 position:63%
Take a bath of liquid helium
below the lambda point

00:54:57.660 --> 00:54:59.520 position:63%
with a heater on one side.

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:03.120 position:63%
- The helium atoms that are
in contact with that hot face-

00:55:03.120 --> 00:55:04.140 position:63%
- Yeah.
- Are gonna gain energy.

00:55:04.140 --> 00:55:05.460 position:63%
They're gonna jump up this ladder.

00:55:05.460 --> 00:55:06.380 position:63%
- Yeah.
- So they're gonna be part

00:55:06.380 --> 00:55:07.770 position:63%
of the normal fluid.
- Right.

00:55:07.770 --> 00:55:12.770 position:63%
- So my normal fluid is gonna
flow away from the heater,

00:55:13.950 --> 00:55:16.410 position:63%
carrying all of the heat and the entropy

00:55:16.410 --> 00:55:18.060 position:63%
that's imparted into the fluid away.

00:55:18.060 --> 00:55:20.400 position:63%
But as the normal fluid flows away-

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:21.287 position:63%
- Yeah.

00:55:21.287 --> 00:55:23.910 position:63%
- The superfluid flows back to replace it.

00:55:23.910 --> 00:55:24.743 position:63%
- Right.

00:55:24.743 --> 00:55:28.560 position:63%
- And the overall density of
the fluid remains constant.

00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:31.220 position:63%
- Sure.
- So this is a two-fluid

00:55:31.220 --> 00:55:33.035 position:63%
thermomechanical counterflow.

00:55:33.035 --> 00:55:34.530 position:63%
They basically flow through each other

00:55:34.530 --> 00:55:35.637 position:63%
like they weren't even there.

00:55:35.637 --> 00:55:37.980 position:63%
- But you can take this one step further.

00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:39.510 position:63%
Imagine changing the temperature

00:55:39.510 --> 00:55:41.220 position:63%
of the heat source periodically.

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:43.260 position:63%
What is going to happen?

00:55:43.260 --> 00:55:44.970 position:63%
Well, the amount of normal fluid created

00:55:44.970 --> 00:55:47.280 position:63%
will also change periodically.

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:49.890 position:63%
So you get these fluctuations
in the normal fluid

00:55:49.890 --> 00:55:51.600 position:63%
and superfluid densities.

00:55:51.600 --> 00:55:54.030 position:63%
They spread out and travel away,

00:55:54.030 --> 00:55:56.700 position:63%
almost like a sort of sound.

00:55:56.700 --> 00:55:59.703 position:63%
- When we think of first
sound, regular sound,

00:55:59.703 --> 00:56:02.640 position:63%
that's density waves in the air driven

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:04.860 position:63%
by tiny pressure gradients.

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:07.320 position:63%
- [Derek] But with a superfluid,
you can get density waves

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:09.510 position:63%
in the normal and superfluid components.

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:11.820 position:63%
And since the normal fluid
carries all the heat,

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:14.520 position:63%
that means you get temperature waves.

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:16.290 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] And they can
spread out like sound waves.

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:18.890 position:63%
So we call temperature waves
in helium second sound.

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:21.090 position:63%
- The crazy thing about it is

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:24.660 position:63%
it's a completely new
mode of heat transport,

00:56:24.660 --> 00:56:27.690 position:63%
unlike anything else
in the universe, right?

00:56:27.690 --> 00:56:29.970 position:63%
Yeah, when I heard that, I
was like, "That's crazy."

00:56:29.970 --> 00:56:31.470 position:63%
So you always think of

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:34.440 position:63%
conduction, convection, and radiation.

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:37.113 position:63%
And now helium gets a fourth.

00:56:38.130 --> 00:56:40.170 position:63%
- This is a combination of second sound

00:56:40.170 --> 00:56:43.050 position:63%
and thermal counterflow,
and it is effective,

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:45.780 position:63%
around a thousand times
more conductive than copper,

00:56:45.780 --> 00:56:48.300 position:63%
which is one of the best
thermal conductors we know of.

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:50.340 position:63%
It finally helps explain one last thing

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:52.350 position:63%
you've been seeing this whole time,

00:56:52.350 --> 00:56:55.233 position:63%
that is why does the
boiling suddenly stop?

00:56:56.970 --> 00:56:58.950 position:63%
Well, above 2.17 Kelvin,

00:56:58.950 --> 00:57:02.160 position:63%
helium boils just like a normal liquid.

00:57:02.160 --> 00:57:04.840 position:63%
- Say, for example, I've
got a bucket of water

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:08.670 position:63%
and I take a red hot
poker and I dump it in.

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:10.560 position:63%
The bubbles aren't gonna
form anywhere in the bucket.

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:12.870 position:63%
They're gonna form around that poker

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:15.390 position:63%
because you've got this
high temperature region

00:57:15.390 --> 00:57:19.110 position:63%
where you've dumped in
enough energy locally

00:57:19.110 --> 00:57:21.030 position:63%
to vaporize the water

00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:22.890 position:63%
and then that's where the
bubbles are gonna form.

00:57:22.890 --> 00:57:25.050 position:63%
The amount of energy it takes to do this

00:57:25.050 --> 00:57:26.910 position:63%
is the latent heat of vaporization.

00:57:26.910 --> 00:57:31.910 position:63%
In water, that is about
2,200 joules per gram.

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:37.500 position:63%
In helium, it's about 23 joules per gram.

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:42.210 position:63%
So if you've got helium
in its liquid state,

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:45.870 position:63%
a tiny thermal fluctuation is enough

00:57:45.870 --> 00:57:48.690 position:63%
to locally vaporize a small pocket,

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:49.860 position:63%
which then forms a bubble.

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:52.140 position:63%
And that's why boiling is so constant,

00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:53.700 position:63%
because tiny thermal fluctuations

00:57:53.700 --> 00:57:55.653 position:63%
will form bubbles very, very easily.

00:57:57.180 --> 00:58:00.480 position:63%
- But as you cool helium
down below 2.17 kelvin,

00:58:00.480 --> 00:58:02.580 position:63%
thermal counterflows take over.

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:04.260 position:63%
They transfer heat so effectively

00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:06.990 position:63%
that the entire liquid almost immediately

00:58:06.990 --> 00:58:08.730 position:63%
reaches the same temperature.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:10.110 position:63%
So there are no hotspots

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:12.660 position:63%
and no places for bubbles to form,

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:14.883 position:63%
the boiling suddenly stops.

00:58:16.260 --> 00:58:17.850 position:63%
This lack of boiling paired

00:58:17.850 --> 00:58:20.040 position:63%
with its unparalleled heat transfer

00:58:20.040 --> 00:58:22.740 position:63%
also makes superfluid
helium the ideal coolant

00:58:22.740 --> 00:58:24.030 position:63%
at one or two Kelvin,

00:58:24.030 --> 00:58:27.630 position:63%
and that's why CERN uses
around 700,000 liters of it

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:31.293 position:63%
to cool the LHC's 9,000
superconducting magnets.

00:58:32.730 --> 00:58:37.110 position:63%
- There is still one thing
that bothers me though, Chris,

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:40.290 position:63%
which is that, you know,
if you take any substance

00:58:40.290 --> 00:58:42.993 position:63%
and you keep cooling it
down, it always freezes.

00:58:44.220 --> 00:58:46.320 position:63%
But helium never does,

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:49.980 position:63%
yet it does liquefy, which
is the annoying part.

00:58:49.980 --> 00:58:51.480 position:63%
So because it liquefies,

00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:54.357 position:63%
it means that there is some
attraction between the atoms.

00:58:54.357 --> 00:58:55.740 position:63%
And so you'd expect, you know,

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:58.710 position:63%
I can just keep cooling it
down more and more and more

00:58:58.710 --> 00:59:00.480 position:63%
until eventually the kinetic energy

00:59:00.480 --> 00:59:03.030 position:63%
becomes arbitrarily small

00:59:03.030 --> 00:59:05.910 position:63%
and like the attractive
energy must be much larger

00:59:05.910 --> 00:59:07.860 position:63%
and so it must form a solid.

00:59:07.860 --> 00:59:12.480 position:63%
- Yes, so this is the
kind of intuitive picture

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:13.410 position:63%
that you would expect.

00:59:13.410 --> 00:59:17.130 position:63%
Well, you can't answer
that in an intuitive way.

00:59:17.130 --> 00:59:19.770 position:63%
You have to bring in quantum mechanics.

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:21.390 position:63%
- [Derek] Because according
to quantum mechanics,

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:24.000 position:63%
the more precisely you
know a particle's position,

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:27.000 position:63%
the less you know about its
momentum and vice versa.

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:29.610 position:63%
This is known as Heisenberg's
uncertainty principle,

00:59:29.610 --> 00:59:31.290 position:63%
and mathematically it tells you

00:59:31.290 --> 00:59:33.420 position:63%
that the uncertainty in position delta x

00:59:33.420 --> 00:59:35.880 position:63%
times the uncertainty in momentum delta p

00:59:35.880 --> 00:59:38.820 position:63%
is greater than or
equal to h bar over two.

00:59:38.820 --> 00:59:42.030 position:63%
For simplicity, let's just
say it's around h bar.

00:59:42.030 --> 00:59:45.300 position:63%
- Let's say I've got an atom in a liquid

00:59:45.300 --> 00:59:46.920 position:63%
and it has some freedom.

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:50.220 position:63%
It can move around, but you
know, it's very closely packed in

00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:51.900 position:63%
with all of its neighboring atoms.

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:55.380 position:63%
Let's say that it occupies
some spherical volume

00:59:55.380 --> 00:59:56.673 position:63%
with a radius r.

00:59:57.570 --> 01:00:00.870 position:63%
Well, we can say that
that r is the uncertainty

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:01.803 position:63%
in its position.

01:00:02.670 --> 01:00:05.100 position:63%
So if the uncertainty in the momentum

01:00:05.100 --> 01:00:10.100 position:63%
is gonna look like h-bar,
Planck's constant, over r.

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:13.110 position:63%
- [Derek] But if it's got
an uncertainty in momentum,

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:16.680 position:63%
it means it must be jiggling
around inside that sphere.

01:00:16.680 --> 01:00:20.100 position:63%
- We know if it's jiggling,
we can say it's got an energy.

01:00:20.100 --> 01:00:21.870 position:63%
Well, we know what it's momentum is.

01:00:21.870 --> 01:00:24.933 position:63%
It's Planck's constant over r.

01:00:25.860 --> 01:00:27.660 position:63%
So how do we get the
energy? Well, it's easy.

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:30.543 position:63%
We say it's the momentum
squared over twice the mass.

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:35.490 position:63%
Now, helium being such a light atom,

01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:38.190 position:63%
that mass term is very small.

01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:40.500 position:63%
So that means that the energy that it has,

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:44.730 position:63%
because it's confined,
is very, very large.

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:47.040 position:63%
Now, this is what we call
the zero point energy.

01:00:47.040 --> 01:00:49.380 position:63%
This is the energy it cannot get rid of.

01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:51.330 position:63%
It's nothing to do with thermal energy.

01:00:51.330 --> 01:00:53.610 position:63%
It's not because there's a
temperature in the liquid.

01:00:53.610 --> 01:00:56.850 position:63%
In fact, when you're in the liquid state,

01:00:56.850 --> 01:01:00.330 position:63%
that zero point energy
accounts for more than 98%

01:01:00.330 --> 01:01:01.980 position:63%
of the total energy in the fluid.

01:01:03.180 --> 01:01:04.106 position:63%
- Wait, what?

01:01:04.106 --> 01:01:05.493 position:63%
- [Dr. Chris] It is enormous.

01:01:05.493 --> 01:01:07.650 position:63%
- That is crazy. I had no clue.

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:10.560 position:63%
- [Derek] This zero
point energy is too large

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:13.170 position:63%
for the atoms ever to lock down in place.

01:01:13.170 --> 01:01:17.490 position:63%
So helium never freezes
just by cooling it down.

01:01:17.490 --> 01:01:20.430 position:63%
- So when you're thinking of
this kind of ladder of states,

01:01:20.430 --> 01:01:22.260 position:63%
this kind of BEC picture, like, you know,

01:01:22.260 --> 01:01:25.470 position:63%
everything's macroscopically
occupying this bottom state,

01:01:25.470 --> 01:01:29.850 position:63%
we think, the intuition is
to think of it as like ladder

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:33.210 position:63%
where each rung is nice and evenly spaced.

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:34.110 position:63%
But the way to think of it

01:01:34.110 --> 01:01:35.643 position:63%
in terms of this zero point energy is that

01:01:35.643 --> 01:01:38.103 position:63%
that first rung is 10
stories off the floor.

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:42.770 position:63%
And then the thermal states
stack up on top of that.

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:45.240 position:63%
- Oh, that's so good.

01:01:45.240 --> 01:01:47.790 position:63%
- But there is one way in
which you can freeze helium,

01:01:47.790 --> 01:01:50.160 position:63%
which was discovered by Onnes's successor.

01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:52.950 position:63%
That is by cooling helium
down to about one Kelvin

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:56.340 position:63%
and by increasing the
pressure to 25 atmospheres.

01:01:56.340 --> 01:01:59.490 position:63%
If you do that, helium finally freezes.

01:01:59.490 --> 01:02:04.200 position:63%
As for Onnes, he died
in 1926 at the age of 72.

01:02:04.200 --> 01:02:07.890 position:63%
He had been making superfluid
helium for 18 years,

01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:10.230 position:63%
looking at it, seeing hint after hint,

01:02:10.230 --> 01:02:11.850 position:63%
and yet he never realized

01:02:11.850 --> 01:02:14.790 position:63%
what he'd been staring at the entire time.

01:02:14.790 --> 01:02:15.930 position:63%
Now, you might look at this

01:02:15.930 --> 01:02:19.380 position:63%
and think Onnes was a little
stupid or ignorant to miss it

01:02:19.380 --> 01:02:21.210 position:63%
and to not put the pieces together,

01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:22.770 position:63%
but I don't think that's the case.

01:02:22.770 --> 01:02:25.530 position:63%
Instead, I think it reveals
a fundamental feature

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:26.970 position:63%
of being human.

01:02:26.970 --> 01:02:29.310 position:63%
I think it goes to show
a lot of people think

01:02:29.310 --> 01:02:30.570 position:63%
you got to see it to believe it.

01:02:30.570 --> 01:02:35.490 position:63%
And I think often you have to
believe it in order to see it.

01:02:35.490 --> 01:02:38.377 position:63%
It makes me think a lot about Kuhn's

01:02:38.377 --> 01:02:41.310 position:63%
"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions,"

01:02:41.310 --> 01:02:43.050 position:63%
because it focuses a lot on,

01:02:43.050 --> 01:02:44.610 position:63%
you know, you have the existing science,

01:02:44.610 --> 01:02:45.690 position:63%
you have the existing paradigms,

01:02:45.690 --> 01:02:46.920 position:63%
you have the existing formulas

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:49.440 position:63%
that these things are supposed to follow.

01:02:49.440 --> 01:02:53.640 position:63%
And how often would the data
point going off the line

01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:56.580 position:63%
just be some error, someone
messed up, condensation,

01:02:56.580 --> 01:02:58.110 position:63%
just something happening?

01:02:58.110 --> 01:02:59.910 position:63%
It's easy to write those things off.

01:02:59.910 --> 01:03:01.950 position:63%
So I have a lot of sympathy

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:03.810 position:63%
and I don't think, "Oh,
how could they miss it?"

01:03:03.810 --> 01:03:06.930 position:63%
I kind of think like, "No,
that's actually how we operate."

01:03:06.930 --> 01:03:08.520 position:63%
Especially, you know,

01:03:08.520 --> 01:03:09.840 position:63%
I don't know if scientists are supposed

01:03:09.840 --> 01:03:11.100 position:63%
to be a little bit more observant

01:03:11.100 --> 01:03:13.650 position:63%
or a little bit more skeptical,
but at the same time,

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:16.440 position:63%
you know, you're up against
so much chaos in the world

01:03:16.440 --> 01:03:21.007 position:63%
that you do kind of sometimes just see,

01:03:21.007 --> 01:03:23.640 position:63%
"Yeah, yeah, that fits the,
yeah, that fits the data,

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:25.380 position:63%
you know, like that's, you know",

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:27.660 position:63%
and they sort of ignore
things that don't fit

01:03:27.660 --> 01:03:29.040 position:63%
because you're looking for something.

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:30.630 position:63%
I don't know.
- Yeah.

01:03:30.630 --> 01:03:34.170 position:63%
But I think probably especially
if you are a scientist,

01:03:34.170 --> 01:03:36.660 position:63%
it's these unexpected things

01:03:36.660 --> 01:03:38.910 position:63%
that can end up having
the biggest breakthrough

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:40.740 position:63%
because those are the things that,

01:03:40.740 --> 01:03:44.100 position:63%
you know, disconfirm
your view of reality.

01:03:44.100 --> 01:03:46.530 position:63%
- And the thing is, often all the signs

01:03:46.530 --> 01:03:48.090 position:63%
for the breakthrough are there,

01:03:48.090 --> 01:03:50.190 position:63%
it's just that we're
not ready to see them.

01:03:50.190 --> 01:03:54.240 position:63%
So it makes you wonder what
signs are we missing right now?

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:55.380 position:63%
You know, I used to think

01:03:55.380 --> 01:03:57.450 position:63%
that the reason someone
didn't make the breakthrough

01:03:57.450 --> 01:03:59.270 position:63%
was because maybe they
weren't smart enough

01:03:59.270 --> 01:04:02.460 position:63%
or they didn't work hard enough
or didn't get lucky enough.

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:05.220 position:63%
But now I feel like I've
always missed something.

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:07.890 position:63%
Just look at Onnes. He
had all of those things.

01:04:07.890 --> 01:04:10.920 position:63%
He was an experimental genius,
had an immense work ethic,

01:04:10.920 --> 01:04:12.990 position:63%
and even discovered superconductivity,

01:04:12.990 --> 01:04:15.990 position:63%
one of the biggest breakthroughs
of the 20th century.

01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:18.060 position:63%
He got three out of three.

01:04:18.060 --> 01:04:21.450 position:63%
So perhaps sometimes making the
breakthrough doesn't require

01:04:21.450 --> 01:04:24.750 position:63%
any extra intelligence,
hard work or even luck.

01:04:24.750 --> 01:04:27.660 position:63%
Maybe all it takes is for us to let go

01:04:27.660 --> 01:04:29.760 position:63%
of the way we believe the world to be

01:04:29.760 --> 01:04:31.590 position:63%
and instead, just for a moment,

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:33.990 position:63%
try to see it as it really is.

01:04:33.990 --> 01:04:36.450 position:63%
What if that is all it takes?

01:04:36.450 --> 01:04:39.900 position:63%
And Onnes, well, he may
have missed superfluidity,

01:04:39.900 --> 01:04:42.780 position:63%
but he changed cold
temperature physics forever.

01:04:42.780 --> 01:04:45.450 position:63%
And in 1913, he received
the Nobel Prize in physics

01:04:45.450 --> 01:04:48.480 position:63%
for his achievements at
finding out what happens

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:50.433 position:63%
when you just keep cooling down.

01:04:51.296 --> 01:04:54.420 position:63%
(logo whooshing)

01:04:54.420 --> 01:04:56.070 position:63%
Hey, two quick final things.

01:04:56.070 --> 01:04:57.870 position:63%
First, this video would
not have been possible

01:04:57.870 --> 01:04:59.790 position:63%
without the help of
Professor Weinfurtner's

01:04:59.790 --> 01:05:02.070 position:63%
Gravity Laboratory at
Nottingham University

01:05:02.070 --> 01:05:03.570 position:63%
and the funding provided to them

01:05:03.570 --> 01:05:05.610 position:63%
to run their superfluid experiments.

01:05:05.610 --> 01:05:07.830 position:63%
So I want to give a big
thank you to all of them

01:05:07.830 --> 01:05:10.980 position:63%
for helping us recreate these experiments.

01:05:10.980 --> 01:05:13.230 position:63%
The second thing is
that we didn't even get

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:14.670 position:63%
to show you their research

01:05:14.670 --> 01:05:17.040 position:63%
because while it might
seem like we've solved

01:05:17.040 --> 01:05:18.540 position:63%
all the mysteries of superfluids,

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:20.820 position:63%
it turns out we're just getting started

01:05:20.820 --> 01:05:23.010 position:63%
because really weird things happen

01:05:23.010 --> 01:05:24.840 position:63%
when you start rotating them.

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:27.960 position:63%
And this rotation or the
physics of it can play a role

01:05:27.960 --> 01:05:29.520 position:63%
in everything from neutron stars

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:34.110 position:63%
to simulating rotating black
holes to a black hole bomb.

01:05:34.110 --> 01:05:36.030 position:63%
And because if you look closely enough,

01:05:36.030 --> 01:05:38.370 position:63%
you can kind of see the entire universe

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:40.140 position:63%
in a drop of superfluid.

01:05:40.140 --> 01:05:43.166 position:63%
So let us know if you want us
to make a part two on that.

